e grillestep notechancolor? zoom player inport outport deltapict cmnpref staff fontsize approx approx?)) (list 1 0 nil (quote t) (quote :normal) 1 0 (om-make-point 400 20) (om-make-point 370 280) 0 1000 nil 1 (quote :fluidsynth) nil nil (om-make-point 0 0) (let ((newobj (make-instance (quote edition-values)))) (setf (paper-size newobj) (om-make-point 600 800)) (setf (top-margin newobj) 2) (setf (left-margin newobj) 1) (setf (right-margin newobj) 1) (setf (bottom-margin newobj) 1) (setf (orientation newobj) nil) (setf (scale newobj) nil) (setf (system-space newobj) (quote (1))) (setf (system-color newobj) (quote nil)) (setf (line-space newobj) 1) (setf (title newobj) nil) (setf (show-title? newobj) nil) (setf (show-page? newobj) nil) (setf (sheet-id newobj) nil) (setf (page-mode newobj) nil) newobj) (quote g) 24 2 nil)) t nil nil nil (quote nil)))) (when (fboundp (quote set-active)) (set-active box nil)) box) (let ((box (om-load-boxcomment "The rule follow-profile-hr heuristically
constrains the pitches or rhythmic values
of the chosen voice to follow an input
profile. The profile can be defined by a list,
a score (or part or voice), or a BPF. With
the switch at (1) you can switch between
three such cases.
As there are no additional rules here for
simplicity, the result follows the given
profile directly. Try changing the inputs
(e.g., the list of numbers) to see how the
result reacts. You can also change which
voice to follow from the given score (2), 0
is the highest voice and so forth. 
Only the first n values are affected (3). If n
is 0 then all values of a list or notes from a
voice are taken. However, a BPF would
have no effect then (n is used to sample
the BPF).
By default, the solution follows the profile
directly. Alternatively, only the intervals or
only the interval directions are followed
(4).   
You can also use this rule for constraining
the rhythm (select the mode, 5), but for
using that, other settings in this patch
should change as well (e.g., you would
need to define some larger domain of
rhythmic values, currently only
quarter-notes are created). 
The profile can also start only later in the
music, controlled by the optional argument
start (5)." (om-make-point 280 584) (quote "The rule follow-profile-hr heuristically$constrains the pitches or rhythmic values$of the chosen voice to follow an input$profile. The profile can be defined by a list,$a score (or part or voice), or a BPF. With$the switch at (1) you can switch between$three such cases.$$As there are no additional rules here for$simplicity, the result follows the given$profile directly. Try changing the inputs$(e.g., the list of numbers) to see how the$result reacts. You can also change which$voice to follow from the given score (2), 0$is the highest voice and so forth. $$Only the first n values are affected (3). If n$is 0 then all values of a list or notes from a$voice are taken. However, a BPF would$have no effect then (n is used to sample$the BPF).$$By default, the solution follows the profile$directly. Alternatively, only the intervals or$only the interval directions are followed$(4).   $$You can also use this rule for constraining$the rhythm (select the mode, 5), but for$using that, other settings in this patch$should change as well (e.g., you would$need to define some larger domain of$rhythmic values, currently only$quarter-notes are created). $$The profile can also start only later in the$music, controlled by the optional argument$start (5).") "" (om-make-point 707 60) nil (om-make-color 0 0 0) (om-make-font "Verdana" 12 :family "Verdana" :style (quote (:plain)) :mode (quote nil))))) (when (fboundp (quote set-active)) (set-active box nil)) box) (let ((box (om-load-boxcomment "2" (om-make-point 31 45) (quote "2") "" (om-make-point 522 107) nil (om-make-color 0.4261512 0.610573 0.58903295) (om-make-font "Verdana" 24.0D0 :family "Verdana" :style (quote (:bold)) :mode (quote nil))))) (when (fboundp (quote set-active)) (set-active box nil)) box) (let ((box (om-load-boxcomment "3" (om-make-point 31 46) (quote "3") "" (om-make-point 274 362) nil (om-make-color 0.4261512 0.610573 0.58903295) (om-make-font "Verdana" 24.0D0 :family "Verdana" 